 uh is a driver of  european growth but that said berlin is  showing more flexibility on a possible  oil embargo than on gas germany's  economy minister has been working around  the clock to wean germany off of russian  fossil fuels and he thinks that will be  largely accomplished in the case of coal  and oil by the end of this year it's  also easier to find other suppliers for  oil so i think there when the eu foreign  policy chief says that everything is on  the table and that includes oil and gas  he's certainly speaking for germany as  well  so melinda what would need to happen for  germany to  accept a serious hit to the economy and  change their stance about  russian gas  well we have seen germany change its  position on the nordstrom 2 gas pipeline  from russia and on weapons deliveries in  response to rising pressure for example  on the weapons deliveries uh those  images coming out of butcher showing  what look like war crimes to have been  committed so i think  certainly germany is under pressure and  we are seeing the potential for some  momentum  there's also been talk about a split in  the cabinet over happy uh heavy weapons  deliveries  for ukraine  what are the different viewpoints there  well as i mentioned schultz and his  government have been criticized for not  being significantly more proactive three  days after the war began he declared  that germany had entered a new era and  broke with a long-standing taboo to send  defensive weapons to ukraine but in  recent weeks we've seen stalling and  equivocation when it comes to kiev's  repeated calls for heavier weapons the  chancellor repeatedly dodging questions  on the topic even as recently as this  weekend and that despite the fact that  there are dozens of tanks for example  sitting around unused needing  maintenance on the site of the ryan  matthau company which would in fact like  to send them to ukraine that's the kind  of creative solution that the foreign  minister means when she depends dem